 "present", "mechanism": "regime_switch", "entities": [384, 716, 946, 948, 1116, 1121, 1304, 1464, 1572, 1635, 1740, 1955], "window": [33806, 63806], "confidence": 0.93, "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_01", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "The group underwent an abrupt regime switch at t\u224848036. Neighbourhood turnover jumps from a ~0.42 baseline to a saturated 1.0 over roughly one bandwidth and then collapses to ~0.06, and a direct before/after diff with a 5000-unit half-window yields Jaccard 0.0 (146 old edges dropped, 94 new edges formed, no overlap) against control cuts of 0.445 and 0.461 elsewhere in the period. The old contact cohort (98, 258, 292, 927, 1020, 1155, 1179, 1247, 1848) fires 335 events before and 2 after; the new cohort (117, 205, 295, 1045, 1184, 1257, 1460, 1638, 1950) fires 11 before and 330 after \u2014 the two partner sets are almost perfectly time-disjoint. Alternative explanations fail: group event volume and per-node degree are essentially unchanged across the boundary (~45 events, ~15-17 partners on each side), so this is rewiring rather than a burst or a popularity effect; global traffic peaks before the change point rather than at it; and a peripheral control group shows uniformly high turnover with no step, confirming the group's own spike-and-return profile is the discriminating signal."}, "raw_final_response": "Evidence is conclusive. Key findings:\n\n**Sharp transition at t\u224848036:**\n- Turnover baseline ~0.42 during 39k\u201345k, rises steeply 0.59\u21920.64\u21920.67\u21920.95\u2192**1.00** at t=48036, holds at 1.0 through 49575, then collapses to **0.06** by 52652 (step_01). A single spike with a stable low plateau afterward \u2014 the signature of one discrete rewiring, not gradual drift.\n- Direct snapshot comparison across the change point gives **Jaccard = 0.0** (146 edges removed, 94 added, zero overlap; step_03), versus **0.445** and **0.461** at equally-spaced control cut points before and after (steps 04, 05).\n\n**Partner sets are time-segregated, not merely reordered:**\n- Old partners {98,258,292,927,1020,1155,1179,1247,1848}: 335 events before, **2** after (step_06).\n- New partners {117,205,295,1045,1184,1257,1460,1638,1950}: 11 events before, **330** after (step_07).\n\n**Confounders ruled out:**\n- *Volume*: group activity is flat (~75\u201393 events/bin) on both sides (step_02); graph-wide traffic peaks at 41.8k\u201345.8k, *before* the change point, so the switch does not track global activity (step_09).\n- *Degree/popularity*: group members hold ~15\u201317 distinct partners and 45\u201347 events both before (step_12) and after (step_11) \u2014 identical connectivity, entirely different counterparties. Pure rewiring.\n- *Baseline turnover*: a comparison group of peripheral nodes sits at ~0.95\u20131.0 change throughout with no step structure (step_13), confirming 1.0 alone is not distinctive \u2014 what matters is the group's jump from its own 0.42 baseline and return to 0.06.\n- *Time-ignoring connection*: edge 384\u21921257 exists in the full window (step_14) but its 3 events all fall in the post-switch regime, showing static connectivity would mask the split.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_cd75e8ee\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [384, 716, 946, 948, 1116, 1121, 1304, 1464, 1572, 1635, 1740, 1955],\n  \"window\": [33806, 63806],\n  \"confidence\": 0.93,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The group underwent an abrupt regime switch at t\u224848036. Neighbourhood turnover jumps from a ~0.42 baseline to a saturated 1.0 over roughly one bandwidth and then collapses to ~0.06, and a direct before/after diff with a 5000-unit half-window yields Jaccard 0.0 (146 old edges dropped, 94 new edges formed, no overlap) against control cuts of 0.445 and 0.461 elsewhere in the period. The old contact cohort (98, 258, 292, 927, 1020, 1155, 1179, 1247, 1848) fires 335 events before and 2 after; the new cohort (117, 205, 295, 1045, 1184, 1257, 1460, 1638, 1950) fires 11 before and 330 after \u2014 the two partner sets are almost perfectly time-disjoint. Alternative explanations fail: group event volume and per-node degree are essentially unchanged across the boundary (~45 events, ~15-17 partners on each side), so this is rewiring rather than a burst or a popularity effect; global traffic peaks before the change point rather than at it; and a peripheral control group shows uniformly high turnover with no step, confirming the group's own spike-and-return profile is the discriminating signal.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 52811, "total_output_tokens": 4971